PLACES=[
N('on-a-slant-village-mandan','On-A-Slant Indian Village','Mandan','nd','town','traces',46.7672,-100.8468,'site','public','walk','','about 1575–1781',
  'A Mandan earthlodge town where the Heart River meets the Missouri, built on ground sloping toward the river, which gave it its name. The park gives it about 85 earthlodges and some 1,500 people; it was fortified with a ditch and palisade. Smallpox in 1781 emptied it, and Lewis and Clark found it in ruins in 1804. The Civilian Conservation Corps rebuilt lodges here in the 1930s.',
  'Six reconstructed earthlodges, one of them a large council lodge, stand on the old village ground (the park page; Wikipedia credits the CCC with the first replicas). The rest of the village is the ground itself: the bare-ground LiDAR in the pin check shows dozens of round lodge pits round the reconstructions, but they were not seen on the ground. Self-guided tours in summer.',
  'A Mandan town on the slope, emptied by smallpox twenty years before Lewis and Clark walked through it.',
  FALACC+' The lodges are in the valley below the visitor centre; the nearest mapped lot is on Fort Lincoln Road about 180 m south.',
  [FAL,W('Fort Abraham Lincoln State Park'),HM(153125,'Slant Indian Village')],brick='no',
  th=[46.76567,-100.84602],thn='Lot on Fort Lincoln Road, by the visitor centre'),
N('fort-mckeen-blockhouses-mandan','Fort McKeen infantry post and blockhouses','Mandan','nd','fort','traces',46.76956,-100.85418,'site','public','walk','','1872–1891',
  'The first post here, built in June 1872 by two companies of the 6th Infantry on the bluff above On-A-Slant to guard the Northern Pacific surveyors, and renamed Fort Abraham Lincoln that November. Once the cavalry post was built on the flat below in 1873 both garrisons carried the name; late in the fort\'s life the infantry buildings were taken down and the lumber moved below.',
  'Rebuilt blockhouses on the bluff, with cornerstone markers laid out by the CCC where the barracks, officers\' quarters, hospital and other buildings stood, and signs on the Young Hawk interpretive trail. The date of the rebuilt blockhouses is not settled: one HMdb marker says 1933, another says the CCC rebuilt them and the palisades in 1935, and Wikipedia puts the CCC work at 1934.',
  '',
  FALACC+' The blockhouses are at the north end of the park road, by the Young Hawk Interpretive Loop lot.',
  [FAL,HM(262756,'Fort Abraham Lincoln Infantry Post'),HM(227861,'Blockhouses & Palisades'),HM(153201,'Officers\' Quarters'),W('Fort Abraham Lincoln State Park')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',th=[46.76950,-100.85518],thn='Young Hawk Interpretive Loop lot'),
N('fort-abraham-lincoln-cavalry-post','Fort Abraham Lincoln cavalry post and Custer House','Mandan','nd','fort','traces',46.75926,-100.84716,'exact','public','walk','burned','1873–1891',
  'The cavalry post of 1873, where the 7th Cavalry under Lt. Col. George A. Custer was stationed until the 1876 campaign that ended at the Little Bighorn. With about 650 men in 1874 it was among the largest posts on the northern plains, with 78 buildings at its height. It closed in 1891, and settlers took the buildings apart for their lumber and nails. Custer\'s first house burned in February 1874 and was rebuilt that summer.',
  'Nothing original stands. The Custer House is a 1989 reconstruction of the second, 1874 house, built from old records and an archaeological survey of the foundation, and shown on living-history tours in summer; the park says reconstructed barracks, granary, commissary and stables stand too, and CCC cornerstones mark where other buildings were. On the aerial the house stands in a north-south row of square outlines, the rest of Officers\' Row.',
  'Custer\'s house, burned and rebuilt in 1874, rebuilt again for the state centennial.',
  FALACC+' The cavalry post is on the flat south of the visitor centre; the nearest mapped lot is on the Bloody Knives Loop, about 230 m south of the house.',
  [FAL,HM(96771,'Commanding Officer\'s Quarters'),W('Fort Abraham Lincoln State Park')],brick='?',
  th=[46.75722,-100.84721],thn='Bloody Knives Loop lot'),
N('huff-indian-village','Huff Indian Village','Huff','nd','town','traces',46.6187,-100.6425,'site','public','walk','','about 1450',
  'A planned, fortified Mandan town of about AD 1450, perhaps home to a thousand people and probably lived in for only about twenty years. It is a National Historic Landmark; the Army Corps of Engineers armoured the river bank in the 1960s to save it, so most of it has never been dug.',
  'The whole town plan shows on the surface: depressions of more than a hundred rectangular lodges in rows along the river, a central plaza, and a fortification ditch more than 2,000 feet long with ten bastions round about twelve acres. Interpretive signs make a walking tour.',
  'A whole town plan from 1450 still drawn on the prairie.',
  'State Historical Society of North Dakota state historic site, half a mile south of Huff off ND 1806; open year round. '+SHSFREE+' Parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [SH('huff','Huff Indian Village'),W('Huff Archeological Site'),HM(154135,'Huff Indian Village State Historic Site')],brick='no'),
N('double-ditch-indian-village','Double Ditch Indian Village','Bismarck','nd','town','traces',46.9365,-100.9009,'site','public','walk','','1490–1785',
  'A large Mandan earthlodge town on the east bluff of the Missouri, lived in for nearly 300 years and one of seven to nine Mandan towns near the mouth of the Heart River. The smallpox epidemic of about 1781 emptied it. It is named for its two visible fortification ditches; archaeologists found two more outside them.',
  'Earthlodge depressions, the two fortification ditches and more than 30 midden mounds one to ten feet high round the village, with walking paths, a run of interpretive signs and a Depression-era stone shelter (separately listed on the National Register in 2014). No facilities.',
  '',
  'State Historical Society of North Dakota state historic site on ND 1804, seven and a half miles north of Bismarck; open year round. '+SHSFREE+' Parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [SH('doubleditch','Double Ditch'),W('Double Ditch'),HM(154710,'Double Ditch State Historic Site')],brick='no'),
N('menoken-indian-village','Menoken Indian Village','Menoken','nd','town','traces',46.8412,-100.5182,'site','public','walk','','about 1200',
  'A small fortified village of about 30 oval earth-covered houses on a terrace above Apple Creek, radiocarbon dated to about AD 1200 and built by Late Plains Woodland people. It was made a National Historic Landmark in 1964 as a supposed Mandan village of the explorers\' time; it is now known to be far older.',
  'The fortification ditch still shows: an arc about 245 m long with four bastion loops, about 1.5 m deep and 6 to 7 m wide, round about one and a half acres (Wikipedia; the SHSND page says the ditch can still be seen). Interpretive signs make a walking tour. Depression-era work-relief features on the site are separately listed on the National Register.',
  '',
  'State Historical Society of North Dakota state historic site on 171st Street NE, 1.5 miles northeast of Menoken and about 10 miles east of Bismarck; open year round. '+SHSFREE+' Parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [SH('menoken','Menoken Indian Village'),W('Menoken Indian Village Site'),HM(153165,'Menoken Indian Village Site')],brick='no'),
N('chief-lookings-village-bismarck','Chief Looking\'s Village','Bismarck','nd','town','traces',46.8303,-100.8294,'site','public','walk','','about 1550–1600',
  'A Mandan town of about 1550 to 1600 on a bluff on the east side of Pioneer Park, one of a dozen or more round the mouth of the Heart River; it is also called the Ward Earth Lodge Village. The city bought it in 1930, and in 1934 the CCC dug three lodge floors and built dome-shaped copies of them.',
  'The 1978 nomination describes about 43 earthlodge depressions, storage-pit depressions and a moat with two bastion-like bulges round about six acres; the bare-ground LiDAR in the pin check shows faint round hollows across the bluff top, not seen on the ground. A 0.55-mile unpaved interpretive trail goes round it, beside the Pioneer Park Overlook.',
  '',
  'Bismarck Parks and Recreation park at 2023 Burnt Boat Drive, with a parking lot; the gate is open daily from May to October, weather permitting, and the posted hours are 8 am to 10 pm. Where the lot is was not checked on the aerial.',
  [['Bismarck Parks and Recreation: Chief Looking\'s Village','https://www.bisparks.org/parks/chief-lookings-village/'],NR('78001990','Chief Looking\'s Village site'),W('Chief Looking\'s Village site (32BL3)')],brick='no'),
N('camp-hancock-bismarck','Camp Hancock','Bismarck','nd','fort','standing',46.80515,-100.79045,'exact','public','walk','','1872',
  'Part of a post set up as Camp Greeley in 1872 to guard the crews building the Northern Pacific, renamed Camp Hancock in 1873, and garrisoned until 1877. Its log headquarters is the oldest building in Bismarck. The Bread of Life Church of 1881, later St. George\'s Episcopal and called the oldest church in Bismarck, was moved here in 1965.',
  'The 1872 log headquarters still stands, enlarged and remodelled and with its logs hidden under clapboard; it is a small museum. Beside it are the Bread of Life Church and a 1909 Northern Pacific locomotive. The grounds and outdoor signs are open year round; the buildings open only for events, school tours, rentals and by appointment, so the interiors were not checked.',
  '',
  'State Historical Society of North Dakota state historic site at 101 East Main Avenue, Bismarck. '+SHSFREE+' '+TOWN,
  [SH('hancock','Camp Hancock'),W('Camp Hancock State Historic Site'),HM(154545,'Church of the Bread of Life')],brick='no'),
N('former-governors-mansion-bismarck','Former Governors\' Mansion','Bismarck','nd','civic','standing',46.8109,-100.78645,'exact','public','walk','','1884',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey Victorian house built in 1884 for Asa Fisher, banker, liquor dealer and land-office register, and sold to the state for $5,000 in 1893. Twenty governors lived in it until 1960; it then held state health offices, passed to the State Historical Society in 1975 and opened as a museum in 1983.',
  'A clapboard stick-style house with red brick chimneys (Wikipedia photograph), restored to how it may have looked in 1893, with room exhibits on the restoration, patches of exposed wallpaper showing the changes of taste, governors\' furniture, a 1903 carriage house and heritage gardens. Open daily 9 am to 5 pm from the Saturday of Memorial Day weekend to Labor Day; the rest of the year for events and by appointment. Grounds open year round.',
  '',
  'State Historical Society of North Dakota state historic site at 320 East Avenue B, Bismarck. '+SHSFREE+' '+TOWN,
  [SH('fgm','Former Governors\' Mansion'),W('Former North Dakota Executive Mansion')],brick='some'),
N('bismarck-northern-pacific-depot','Northern Pacific Railway Depot','Bismarck','nd','rail','standing',46.805,-100.78528,'site','view','walk','stations','1901',
  'A Mission Revival station built for the Northern Pacific in 1900–1901 to designs by Reed & Stem of St. Paul, on the site of the Sheridan House hotel, which was moved across the street. Amtrak used it until 1979. After a renovation finished in 2018 a brewery opened in it and closed in 2020.',
  'Walls of concrete faced with chips of marble once meant for the Minnesota State Capitol, wire-brushed to a stucco-like finish, and Northern Pacific monad emblems in terracotta on both towers and the gables. The towers lost their domes and cupolas in 1954 and the original red Ludowici tile roof was replaced then (1977 nomination). Inside, the nomination and Wikipedia describe cherry-trimmed offices and mosaic floors; what survives inside now was not checked.',
  '',
  'Privately owned; seen from Main Avenue. Its present tenants and whether it is open to the public were not checked. '+TOWN,
  [NR('77001022','Northern Pacific Railway Depot'),W('Bismarck station')],brick='no'),
N('patterson-hotel-bismarck','Patterson Hotel','Bismarck','nd','hotel','standing',46.80558,-100.78484,'exact','view','walk','hotels','1910–1930s',
  'Opened on New Year\'s Day 1911 as Alexander McKenzie\'s McKenzie Hotel, and the tallest building in Bismarck until the new Capitol of 1934. Edward Patterson bought it and renamed it in 1927. Wikipedia says building went on into the 1930s, which kept it off the tax rolls. It was the Nonpartisan League\'s headquarters and the state\'s unofficial political hub; after the old Capitol burned in 1930 state agencies leased its second floor.',
  'Ten storeys on a concrete frame, with a first storey of cream Bedford stone, a diagonal corner entrance and stone panels lettered with the hotel\'s name (the 1976 nomination says they then read Ed Patterson; the 2009 photograph in the Wikipedia article shows THE McKENZIE). Its brick is dark brown below and tan in the top storeys in that photograph. The nomination also describes a lobby with terra-cotta floor, murals, gilt cornices, Kasota marble wainscot and mahogany, and the Art Deco Peacock Alley bar with a glass-block front. The rooms became senior housing after the hotel closed in the 1970s; the lobby holds the Peacock Alley restaurant (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private building at 422 East Main Avenue; the ground-floor restaurant is open to customers. '+TOWN,
  [NR('76001352','Patterson Hotel'),W('Patterson Hotel')],brick='no'),
N('north-dakota-state-capitol','North Dakota State Capitol','Bismarck','nd','civic','standing',46.8208,-100.7824,'site','public','walk','burned','1931–1934',
  'The 21-storey Art Deco tower, the "Skyscraper on the Prairie", built from 1931 to 1934 for $2 million after the 1883 capitol burned on December 28, 1930. Designed by Joseph Bell DeRemer and William F. Kurke with Holabird and Root of Chicago. Workers were paid 30 cents an hour, and after strikes the grounds were put under martial law in June 1933. The Hebron Brick Company could not promise enough brick, so the architects faced it in Indiana limestone.',
  'The tallest building in the state, with an observation deck on the 18th floor. Guided tours cover the legislative and judicial wings and ride to the tower top. OMB\'s 2025 summer schedule: weekdays at 9, 10 and 11 am and 1, 2 and 3 pm, Saturdays and the summer holidays at noon, 1 and 2 pm, from the week of May 26 to Labor Day; Wikipedia says weekday tours run all year. Self-guided visits are not allowed on weekends or holidays. Interior fabric not checked.',
  'Built in the Depression under martial law after the old capitol burned.',
  'State of North Dakota, Office of Management and Budget, 600 East Boulevard Avenue. Tours are free in the OMB notice. Parking was not checked.',
  [['OMB: Capitol tours, summer 2025','https://omb.nd.gov/news/public-invited-enjoy-beauty-capitol-summer'],W('North Dakota State Capitol'),['SAH Archipedia: Hebron Brick Company','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/15955']],brick='no'),
N('lewis-and-clark-hotel-mandan','Lewis and Clark Hotel','Mandan','nd','hotel','standing',46.82595,-100.89462,'exact','view','walk','hotels','1918',
  'A four-storey, 120-room hotel built in 1918 by former governor Louis B. Hanna, who had bought and razed the Inter-Ocean Hotel on the site; designed by William J. Gage of Fargo. Merchant\'s National Bank opened in its corner.',
  'Brown Hebron brick (brown in the Wikipedia photograph too) with a rug-textured face over a first floor clad in cream Sullivanesque terra-cotta panels on a terra-cotta base made to look like granite, with a terra-cotta cornice and flower squares. A painted sign on the west wall of the southeast wing reads "Hotel Lewis and Clark Family Rates Home of Western Hospitality". The 1983 nomination says the 50 by 75 foot lobby kept its original character, with a mezzanine over the desk and French doors to the dining room. Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private building at 404 West Main Street, in the Mandan Commercial Historic District. '+TOWN,
  [NR('83001938','Lewis and Clark Hotel'),W('Lewis and Clark Hotel')],brick='no'),
N('st-johns-church-hebron','German Evangelical St. Johns Church','Hebron','nd','worship','standing',46.90361,-102.04667,'site','view','drive','','1908',
  'A Gothic Revival church built in 1908 for a German congregation from plans by Hancock Brothers of Fargo, by contractor Henry Bomgarten with stonework by Conrad Klick of Hebron, of brick from the Hebron Brick Company five blocks away. It is now St. John United Church of Christ.',
  'Walls of light brown pressed Hebron brick on a sandstone foundation, stepped buttresses capped in dark brown stone, and lancet and traceried stained-glass windows; the 2000 nomination also records its three bells of 1908 with German inscriptions and the pews of the nave. Whether visitors can go in was not checked.',
  '',
  'An active church on Church Avenue in Hebron; seen from the street. '+TOWN,
  [NR('00001642','German Evangelical St. Johns Church'),W('German Evangelical St. Johns Church (Hebron, North Dakota)')],brick='no'),
N('fort-rice-state-historic-site','Fort Rice','Fort Rice','nd','fort','traces',46.5131,-100.5837,'site','public','walk','','1864–1878',
  'Built by General Alfred Sully in July 1864 as a field base on the Missouri, first of a chain of forts on the northern plains routes. Its first winter garrison included "Galvanized Yankees", Confederate prisoners who had enlisted; 81 men died in the first year, 37 of scurvy. It hosted the 1868 council that led to the Fort Laramie Treaty and was the base for the Yellowstone survey escorts. Abandoned in November 1878; the state bought the site in 1913.',
  'No building survives. Visitors see depressions, foundation lines and the corner markers the WPA set in the 1940s, with a site marker carrying a history and a map of the fort.',
  'Where Confederate prisoners in Union blue spent a scurvy winter on the Missouri.',
  'State Historical Society of North Dakota state historic site, about 30 miles south of Mandan off ND 1806. Parking space, no visitor services. '+SHSFREE+' Hours not given on the page.',
  [SH('rice','Fort Rice'),HM(154080,'Fort Rice')],brick='no'),
N('cannonball-stage-station','Cannonball Stage Station','Carson','nd','town','traces',46.22167,-101.47742,'site','public','drive','','1877–1880',
  'The fifth stop out of Bismarck on the Bismarck-Deadwood stage road to the Black Hills gold fields, built in 1877 by the Northwest Express and Transportation Company at the Cannonball River crossing, 15 miles southeast of Carson in Grant County. It had a barn and a log building and one keeper. The road declined after a shorter route opened from Pierre late in 1880.',
  'The remains of two dugouts and the rectangular outline of a barn (SHSND); the 1974 marker adds that wheel ruts show in the prairie to the southwest and northeast, and that the old crossing was about 150 feet south of the marker. A small park with a hand-pump water fountain and a picnic table.',
  '',
  'State Historical Society of North Dakota state historic site on the east bank of the Cannonball River; the land was given to the state in 1959. '+SHSFREE+' Hours and parking not checked.',
  [SH('cannonballss','Cannonball Stage Station'),HM(153099,'Cannonball Stage Station')],brick='no'),
N('bismarck-deadwood-stage-trail-flasher','Bismarck-Deadwood Stage Trail marker','Flasher','nd','town','traces',46.4587,-101.2192,'area','public','drive','','1877–1880',
  'The 240-mile stage and freight road from the Northern Pacific railhead at Bismarck to Deadwood, opened in April 1877 with 68 passengers on the first coaches; the company ran 26 Concord coaches and 200 teams. Trade fell away after 1880 when the same company opened a shorter line from Pierre.',
  'A roadside marker; the 1962 marker says it stands on the exact route, which runs off north-northeast and south-southwest, and the SHSND says a few wagon ruts are found near it. The ruts were not seen in any picture opened in this session.',
  '',
  'State Historical Society of North Dakota marker at a roadside stop on the north side of ND 21, half a mile east of Flasher. '+SHSFREE,
  [SH('bdst','Bismarck-Deadwood Stage Trail'),HM(153101,'Bismarck to Deadwood Stage Trail')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('steamboat-warehouse-bismarck','Steamboat Warehouse site','Bismarck','nd','water','marker',46.81457,-100.82144,'area','public','drive','','1883–1925',
  'The Northern Pacific\'s 300-foot stone and frame warehouse, where freight passed between trains and Missouri River steamboats, reached by the River Landing Spur. The SHSND dates it 1883; the 1955 marker speaks of river warehouses of the 1870s. Until the rail bridge of 1882 trains crossed the river on the ice in winter and by ferry in summer. Torn down in 1925.',
  'Nothing of the warehouse stands. A fieldstone monument with a plaque on a 3.21-acre site by the river, across River Road from the water treatment plant.',
  '',
  'State Historical Society of North Dakota site on River Road (ND 1804), open year round, with a small parking area on the west side of the road in front of the marker. '+SHSFREE,
  [SH('steamboat','Steamboat Warehouse'),HM(154055,'Bismarck River Warehouse')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
]

